## 2. The financial rules I will protect

These are fundamental. They are not optional implementation preferences.

GHS and USDC are separate liabilities. A customer's GHS wallet is a real GHS liability. Their USDC wallet is a real USDC liability. A display conversion does not create spendable GHS, and a valuation must never be represented as money available for withdrawal or payment.

GHS is the primary Ghana-facing currency. Prices, orders, merchant settlements and normal store transactions should use GHS. USDC has its own legitimate uses, including crypto deposits and withdrawals, foreign-user accounts, selected Susu groups and explicit conversion.

Cross-wallet funding needs a real quote. The proposed SpendPlanner must support the actual shortfall, exact rate, margin, expiry, confirmation and step-up requirements. A user must understand exactly what will leave each wallet before authorising the transaction.

Your Quick MoMo Deposit and smart-resume idea belongs in this architecture. The three-way shortfall choice—USDC conversion, exact-difference deposit or a combination—should be coordinated with a properly persisted `PaymentIntent`. Automatic completion must never happen merely because a deposit arrived. It requires explicit prior authorisation; otherwise, the user must be prompted.

Merchant money must not become personal money. A merchant needs a proper `BusinessGhsWallet` and business ledger liability. Crediting the business owner's personal `User.availableBalance` is not an acceptable settlement architecture.

Payment execution must be authoritative. Exact decimal handling, database transactions, concurrency control, idempotency, step-up authentication, durable state transitions and reconciliation are essential. Unknown provider outcomes cannot be treated as confirmed failures simply to make an operation appear complete.

The authority latch is also important. Once native-GHS authority is activated, a missing database row or reset feature flag must not silently return the platform to USDC behaviour.

